\use{document}

\title{Drop Caps}
\author{The texish engine}
\date{Version 0.24.2}
\maketitle

\section{The default form}

\dropcap{L}{orem} ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod
tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis
nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis
aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat
nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui
officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum.

The default spans two lines: the letter's top sits on the first line's cap height and
its baseline on the second line's baseline, with the opening word continued in small
capitals. The size is measured from the surrounding font, not hard-coded, so the same
command aligns correctly at any body size or leading.

\section{Spanning more lines}

\dropcap[3]{W}{hen} a grander opening is wanted, the optional first argument sets how
many lines the initial spans. The opening lines are carved out around the letter with a
cutout the height of the letter, so the text below it returns to the full measure without
any further markup. Because the cutout reserves that space by vertical band rather than by
line count, a paragraph shorter than the letter carves correctly too: the opening lines of
the following paragraph flow around the letter rather than riding onto it. Ut enim ad minim
veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat.

\section{The gap and a descender}

\dropcap[2][8]{Q}{uod} erat demonstrandum: the second optional argument widens the
gap between the letter and the carved text — eight points here instead of the default
three. A letter with a tail keeps its baseline on the last spanned line, the tail
descending below it exactly as a metal initial would overhang its slug. Sed ut
perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque
laudantium, totam rem aperiam, eaque ipsa quae ab illo inventore veritatis.

\section{A styled initial}

\dropcap{\textcolor{#8b0000}{A}}{nything} the engine can typeset may serve as the
letter: here the initial is coloured through \verb|\textcolor|. The continuation
styling is a macro of its own — redefine \verb|\dropcaptext| to set the opening word
in italics, bold, or plain text instead of the default small capitals. Nemo enim
ipsam voluptatem quia
voluptas sit aspernatur aut odit aut fugit, sed quia consequuntur magni dolores eos
qui ratione voluptatem sequi nesciunt, neque porro quisquam est.
